Export Text File
Saves the contents of the list as a text file (.txt). A text file only saves the contents of the list without any
formatting information.
Remarks
You can also create a text file in a text editor or a spreadsheet application and then open it as a *.txt file in
a Plant Simulation list. Click Save to save the file.
Type in the name with which you want to save the file and select the file type with the encoding you want
to use:
• ANSI is an 8-bit character set that enables you to represent up to 256 characters (0 through 255). The
ANSI character set is a superset of the 7-bit ASCII character set.
• UTF-8 is another encoding of the Unicode character set. Each character is represented by one to three
bytes.
• Unicode is a 16-bit character set that includes almost all of the written languages of the world. Plant
Simulation saves Unicode in UTF-16 encoding. Each character is represented by two bytes.
Export Text File
Plant Simulation Help 11-3831
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When saving or loading a list file as a text file, the settings you selected with the command Text File
Format determine how Plant Simulation saves data and how it interprets that data while opening this
ASCII file.
See also
writeFile [SimTalk] - lists
Export Text File
11-3832 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Export Object File
Saves the list as an object file with the extension *.pslist containing the contents and formatting
information.
Remarks
A Plant Simulation object file contains the contents of the list and, in addition information about Plant
Simulation-specific formatting, such as data types and other formatting of columns and rows, dimension,
column and row index etc. Thus, the list will open with the same properties, as when you open it in
another Plant Simulation simulation model. Other programs do not recognize this proprietary format. Click
Save to save the file.
See also
writeObjectFile [SimTalk]
readFile [SimTalk] - lists
Export Object File
Plant Simulation Help 11-3833
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Export Excel File
Saves the list as a Microsoft Excel worksheet (.xls). The command opens the dialog Save As in which you
select the drive, the file type, and type in a new name for the file to be saved.
Remarks
Click Save to save the file.
Note
Plant Simulation uses Excel as COM-server when reading and writing MS Excel files. For this reason MS
Excel has to be installed on your computer. Use the start option -NativeExcel to continue using the
previous, no longer supported Excel interface .
Note
Plant Simulation only exports integers in the range between -536.870.912 and 536.870.911 in the
required format. Plant Simulation saves values outside of this range as 0.
Plant Simulation exports the data with the settings that you selected for the units. Suppose that you
selected Meter as the length unit for a column of data type Length. Then Plant Simulation exports
the values in meters to Excel and interprets the values as meters when you import them back to Plant
Simulation.
If Plant Simulation reads an MS Excel table, it attempts to convert the values in the individual column to
the data types of the columns in your Plant Simulation table. This only works if you created the columns
on the Excel worksheets so that they only contain a single data type, meaning that, if you, for example,
assign the data type String to a cell of the specified column, the entire column may only be of data type
String.
Export Excel File
11-3834 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Row 0 (zero) is the exception: If the Excel table, which Plant Simulation reads, has a column index, it
interprets row 0 as the column index, and it will not be part of the data type designation of the columns.
When importing data Plant Simulation considers the dimension of the DataTable. Suppose you set the
number of rows to 3 and the number of columns to 10, then Plant Simulation only imports the first 3
rows and the first 10 columns. This way you can improve the performance of large tables if you do not
need all data from the Excel file.
When Plant Simulation exports data to Excel, it converts it like this:
