Create Formula
Activates or deactivates formula mode for the DataTable. A formula can be any expression accessing and
linking other cells of the list or accessing any of the attributes of the objects.
Remarks
When you define a formula, you will use the same notation you use for defining a Method. You can also
insert and read formulas with methods.
1. To activate Formula mode, click the button so that it is selected.
2. To type a formula into a cell, click in it and type the expression in. Instead, you can also type it into
the text box 
  above the list.
Note
If you call one of the distribution functions in the source code of the formula with z_, you have
to type in the random number stream, as formulas never use the random number stream of the
surrounding object.
3. To show the result of the calculation of the formula, i.e., its value, in the cell, press Enter.
4. To show the formula itself in the text box, click in the cell containing the formula.
Note
The data type of the result of a formula has to have the same data type as the cell or the column in
which the cell containing the formula is located.
See also
Calculate Values with a Formula
getFormula [SimTalk]
setFormula [SimTalk]
Create Formula
11-3850 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Activate Column Index
Activates the column index, i.e., the header of the column, of the DataTable. Click the button again to
deactivate the column index and delete the existing contents.
Remarks
Note
If both column index and row index are active, the cell [0,0], i.e., the cell at which column and row index
intersect, counts as part of the column index, not as part of the row index.
To access a cell in a list/table, you can either use the system index, i.e., the number, which Plant
Simulation assigns, to columns and rows, or you can define your own user-defined index, which can be
any meaningful expression.
The advantage of a user-defined index over the standard system index is that assigning names to
columns and rows is more meaningful than the numbers Plant Simulation assigns by default. The user-
defined index orders["urgent","preferred customer"] will tell your co-modelers more than the
system index orders[3,1].
Note
When specifying cells with the expression [3,1] the first value in brackets designates the column, the
second the row.
Activate Column Index
Plant Simulation Help 11-3851
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Both expressions access the same cell. The user-defined index is more meaningful and less susceptible to
errors than the system index. Another advantage becomes clear as soon as you add a column or row to the
table: Plant Simulation increases the system index of succeeding columns by one, while the user-defined
index remains the same and is still valid. Be aware that accessing a user-defined index is slightly slower
than accessing the system index.
You can only define a user-defined index for the data types string, integer, object, and boolean.
Note
If you select the data type integer for the column index, you have to type a number sign/hash # in front
of a user-defined index expression to distinguish it from the system index.
Example
orders["urgent","preferred customer"]
vehicles["truck",#1]
switch["light",true]
plant["Chicago",.building1.drill]
Proceed as follows to set the data type of the column index:
• Click Activate Column Index on the List ribbon tab to activate it.
• Select the column of the column index, i.e., the column with the number 0.
• Click Format on the List ribbon tab. Select the data type from the drop-down list. For the data types
integer, real, and string you can also type in a Format string.
• Select the check box Fast Index Access to gain fast access to the user-defined column indexes.
Activate Column Index
11-3852 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Select the check box Unique Index Key to only allow unique entries in user-defined indexes, depending
on the data type.
Note
Plant Simulation highlights the cell in red and shows a message if you try to type in an expression that
already exists. Plant Simulation does not show a message if you assign an expression, that already
exists, to an index in a Method!
SimTalk
ColumnIndex [SimTalk]
