Format String [text box]
You can restrict the data the user can specify in the window of the list object to certain values by typing
a Format String. The Format String applies to the data types Integer, Real, Length, Weight, Speed,
Acceleration, Money, and String.
Remarks
The Format String does not restrict assignments via Methods.
• For the data type string Plant Simulation differentiates for each digit:
The letter Outputs
A Letters only
U Upper case letters only
X All characters
C Upper case letters and numbers
N Numbers only
L Letters and numbers
I All characters permitted for object names: letters, including umlaut, numbers
and underscore
If you type in AANXU, the first two digits have to be letters, such as a lower case a or an upper case A.
The third digit has to be a number, such as 1, the fourth digit may be any character, such as e, E, 5 or a
special character, such as the question mark ?. The fifth digit has to be an upper case letter, such as B.
This way you set the length of an expression and the format of each single digit.
Type in several terms of your choice, separated by a semicolon, and not followed by a blank to create a
drop-down list in the cell of the formatted range into which the user clicks. The user can then select one
of the terms you typed in from the drop-down list.
Typing in Entry1;Entry2;Random, for example, creates a drop-down list with the commands Entry1,
Entry2, and Random and provides it in the cells you click within the range you formatted.
Note
You have to double-click into the cell where you want to show the drop-down list in the list window to
actually show it there.
Format String [text box]
11-3872 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• For the data type integer, you can type in a number into the text box to limit the number of digits Plant
Simulation shows. If you type in 3, for example, Plant Simulation only shows the first three digits, when
you type in data, such as 123. Typing in a minus character, such as -3, enables you to type in negative
values as well, such as -123.
For the data type integer you can also type in the value color as the format string. If you then
double-click a cell in this column, Plant Simulation opens the Windows dialog Color. You can then select
a color for the background of this cell here. Plant Simulation calculates the RGB value and enters it as an
integer value into the cell, compare makeRGBValue.
• For the data type real, you can type in two numbers, separated by a point, into the text box to restrict
the number of digits before and after the decimal point. If you enter 5.3, for example, the number the
user enters may have 5 digits altogether; three of these digits may be after the decimal point 1.234 for
example. Typing in a minus character, such as -6.3, allow the user to type in negative values also, such
as -123.456.
SimTalk
FormatString [SimTalk] - lists
Format String [text box]
Plant Simulation Help 11-3873
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

